<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Yale J Biol Med</journal-id><journal-title>The Yale Journal of Biology and Medicine</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0044-0086</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1551-4056</issn></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">4446630</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2595099</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">4446630</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Articles</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>A Practical Approach to Quantitate Hepatic Excretory Function <xref ref-type="fn" rid="fn1">1</xref></article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Bircher</surname><given-names>Johannes</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Hacki</surname><given-names>Walter</given-names></name></contrib></contrib-group><author-notes><fn id="fn1"><label>1</label><p>Supported by Swiss Nat. Foundation.</p></fn></author-notes><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><month>09</month><year>1974</year></pub-date><volume>47</volume><issue>3</issue><fpage>196</fpage><lpage>201</lpage><abstract><p>A statistical comparison of different BSP tests was carried out in normal subjects and in patients with various degrees of chronic liver damage. Only the logarithm of the BSP retention correlated linearly with the physiologically more meaningful determination of the maximal excretory capacity of the liver (BSP <italic>Tm</italic>, Wheele's method). A double logarithmic transformation was required to correlate the second exponential component of the BSP plasma disappearance curve (<italic>k</italic><sub>2</sub>) with the BSP <italic>Tm.</italic> When the limitations of these methods are kept in mind, the observed statistical relationships can be used to express hepatic functional deterioration in more physiological terms.</p></abstract></article-meta></front><body><supplementary-material content-type="scanned-pages"><graphic xlink:href="yjbm00154-0056.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="196" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="yjbm00154-0057.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="197" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="yjbm00154-0058.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="198" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="yjbm00154-0059.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="199" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="yjbm00154-0060.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="200" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="yjbm00154-0061.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="201" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/></supplementary-material></body></article>